      "dom_id": "question-21051",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227715/assets/c85e6ee974a59d956367634fdd1d57b65e9d42a980fe1750886527b173f05d32.png)\n\nFind $BM$ given that the area of $△ACB$ is $49\\,{cm}^{2},$ $AM=5\\,cm,$ and $MC=9\\,cm.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)",
      "worked_solution": "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the height is $h=BM,$ and the corresponding base is \n\n$$\nb=AM+MC=14\\,cm.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the height as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 49 & =\\frac{14\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 98 & =14\\cdot h \\\\ h & =\\frac{98}{14} \\\\ h & =7\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $BM=7\\,\\text{cm}.$",
